{
  "about": "Real night photographs by third parties, fetched from Wikimedia Commons under open licences, for testing the plate solver on real pixels. The photos are not in the repository: data/downloads.json lists each file's URL, size, SHA-256, licence and author, and `uv run python -m research.benchmarks.real_photos fetch` fetches them into the shared .cache. This manifest holds no ground truth yet: no photo has a verified solution, and nothing in it was measured from the sky. It holds no coordinate and no capture time either; those stay in the files.",
  "notes": {
    "tiers": "A: a single untracked wide-field exposure from a camera, full of stars. B: a phone photo of the night sky. C: the Moon over a visible horizon at night or twilight, or the Sun over a skyline in daylight. D: a hard negative, a real night or dark photo with no solvable sky, on which a solver must claim nothing.",
    "fileFacts": "mediaKind, widthPx, heightPx, the camera fields and hasCaptureTime, hasUtcOffset and hasPlace are what the repository's ingest readers (services/ingest) read from the file itself; a field the file does not give is absent, never guessed. Numbers keep six significant digits.",
    "hemisphere": "north or south of the equator. hemisphereSource file_metadata: the side the file's own position lies on. uploader_statement: the file holds no position and the uploader's page names where it was taken. Absent when neither says.",
    "statedSubject": "Tags from a closed vocabulary (the 88 IAU constellation names, milky_way, moon, sun, planet, horizon, clouds, city_lights, aircraft, no_sky) for what the uploader's own description or categories say is in the picture, read from the page in statedSubjectSource. It is the uploader's statement, not ground truth: nothing was checked against the sky."
  },
